          "text": "That message means PHP thinks $user is an instance of App\\Controller\\UserController (your controller class), not your entity. Common causes and how to fix:\n\n1) Verify what $user actually is\n- Dump its class: var_dump(get_class($user)); die; (or dump($user) / dd($user) in Symfony).\nThis will show whether you really have an entity instance or the controller.\n\n2) Fix wrong type-hint / wrong use statement\n- The most frequent mistake is type-hinting or importing the wrong User class. Check the top of your controller for a bad use line like:\n  use App\\Controller\\User;    // WRONG if your entity is in App\\Entity\nor a method signature like:\n  public function show(UserController $user) { ... }  // wrong\n- Correct it to reference the entity:\n  use App\\Entity\\User;\n  public function show(User $user) { ... }\n\nOr use the fully-qualified name: public function show(\\App\\Entity\\User $user)\n\n3) Check how $user is assigned\n- Ensure you are not doing something like $user = $this; or assigning a controller object by mistake.\n- If using $this->getUser(), confirm it returns the User entity (it might return a different class or null).\n\n4) Ensure the entity actually has getName()\n- If your User entity uses getUsername() or getFullName() instead of getName(), call the correct method or add getName() to the entity.\n\nExample fix (common case):\nBefore (wrong import):\n  use App\\Controller\\User;\n  public function profile(User $user) {\n      $user->getName(); // fatal: User is controller class\n  }\n\nAfter:\n  use App\\Entity\\User;\n  public function profile(User $user) {\n      $user->getName();\n  }\n\nIf after checking these items you still see the error, paste the controller code (imports and the method where $user is defined/received) and the User entity class and I\u2019ll point out the exact line to change."
